      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.635,
            0.042,
            0.848,
            0.058
          ],
          "content": "How Forces Affect Motion"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": "N 107"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.089,
            0.544,
            0.11
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "6.6 Newton’s Third Law of Motion"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.104,
            0.12,
            0.179,
            0.158
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.113,
            0.722,
            0.166
          ],
          "content": "We have described the behaviour of an object when a net force acts on it. But do you remember learning earlier that at least two objects must interact for a force to come into play?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.755,
            0.084,
            0.946,
            0.158
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.753,
                0.161,
                0.924,
                0.176
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.22: Kicking a ball"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.168,
            0.722,
            0.237
          ],
          "content": "Haven’t you experienced that when you push a ball with your foot, you feel a force applied by the ball on your foot (Fig. 6.22)? It is then natural to ask how both the objects are affected in this process. Newton’s third law addresses this issue."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.748,
            0.181,
            0.941,
            0.219
          ],
          "content": "Force applied Force applied by the girl on by the table on the table the girl"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.245,
            0.389,
            0.264
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 6.5: Let us explore"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.266,
            0.608,
            0.284
          ],
          "content": "1. Locate a chair with wheels and a large heavy table."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.287,
            0.722,
            0.372
          ],
          "content": "2. Sit on the chair with your legs raised above the floor. Now, using both your hands, push the table away from you, i.e., apply a force on the table in the forward direction as shown in Fig. 6.23a. What happens to you? Does the chair you are sitting upon move in the opposite direction?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.789,
            0.219,
            0.932,
            0.353
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.739,
                0.357,
                0.931,
                0.397
              ],
              "content": "Force applied Force applied by the girl on by the table on the tablethe girl"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.375,
            0.722,
            0.427
          ],
          "content": "3. Now, try to pull the table towards you, i.e., apply a force on the table in the direction opposite to that in step 2 (Fig. 6.23b). In which direction does your chair move now?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.429,
            0.721,
            0.498
          ],
          "content": "What conclusion can you draw from this activity? Each time, when you applied a force on the table, the table applied a force upon you in the opposite direction. You may have experienced this in various other situations."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.5,
            0.721,
            0.604
          ],
          "content": "If you are sitting on a bicycle and you want to move forward without pedalling, what do you do (Fig. 6.24)? You push the ground in the backward direction with your feet causing both you and the bicycle to move forward. This is because your feet apply a force on the ground and the ground applies a force on your feet in the opposite direction. This causes you to move forward along with the bicycle."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.784,
            0.398,
            0.913,
            0.515
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.725,
                0.523,
                0.964,
                0.566
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.23: Applying force on a table in the (a) forward direction, (b) reverse direction"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.605,
            0.722,
            0.708
          ],
          "content": "In fact, this is how you walk or run. Notice what you do while walking or running. You push the ground backwards with your feet (Fig. 6.25). The ground applies an opposite force on your feet to make you move forward. The force applied by the ground is in the form of friction. Thus, in this case friction helps you move rather than oppose you."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.783,
            0.573,
            0.945,
            0.693
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.732,
                0.694,
                0.953,
                0.732
              ],
              "content": "Ground pushing the Feet pushing rider along with the the ground bicycle forward backwards"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_20.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.712,
            0.175,
            0.76
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_21.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.181,
            0.721,
            0.479,
            0.741
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Bridging Science and Society"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.755,
            0.691,
            0.911
          ],
          "content": "While walking or running when you push the ground backwards with your foot, i.e., when your foot tries to slide backwards, the force of friction acts in the forward direction. Had there been no force of friction, your foot would have slipped backwards while attempting to push the ground and you would have fallen down. Grooves are made on the soles of footwear to increase the force of friction between the floor and soles. Similarly, treads on tyres of vehicles help increase the force of friction between the tyres and the road. You can now understand, why it is difficult to walk on wet polished floors or ice, or why it is risky to drive on roads covered with water or snow."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.744,
            0.736,
            0.954,
            0.764
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 6.24: Pushing the ground backwards"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.711,
            0.853,
            0.782,
            0.916
          ],
          "content": "Force of friction pushing the person forward"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.71,
            0.77,
